[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mocking and Stubbing in PHP: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In software testing, especially unit testing, we often encounter dependencies on external components or services. These dependencies can make our tests slow, unreliable, or even impossible to run in isolation. Mocking and stubbing are techniques used to isolate the code under test by replacing these dependencies with controlled substitutes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Stubs"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A stub is a simplified replacement for a dependency that provides predefined responses to method calls. Stubs are primarily used to provide known inputs to the code under test, allowing you to verify that the code behaves correctly given those inputs. They are simple and generally don't involve complex behavior verification."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a Stub
class UserGatewayStub {
  public function findUserById(int $id): array {
    if ($id === 1) {
      return ['id' => 1, 'name' => 'John Doe'];
    } else {
      return [];
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `UserGatewayStub` always returns a predefined user array when `findUserById(1)` is called. This allows you to test code that relies on the `findUserById` method without actually interacting with a real database or external service."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Mocks"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A mock object is a more sophisticated replacement for a dependency. Unlike stubs, mocks are used to verify that the code under test interacts with the dependency in the expected way. Mocks allow you to define expectations about which methods will be called, how many times they will be called, and with what arguments. If these expectations are not met, the test will fail."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Example using PHPUnit's mocking framework
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class UserServiceTest extends TestCase {
  public function testUpdateUserName() {
    $userGateway = $this->createMock(UserGateway::class);

    $userGateway->expects($this->once())
                ->method('updateUserName')
                ->with(1, 'Jane Doe');

    $userService = new UserService($userGateway);
    $userService->updateUserName(1, 'Jane Doe');
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we're using PHPUnit's mocking framework to create a mock `UserGateway` object. We're setting an expectation that the `updateUserName` method will be called exactly once with the arguments `1` and `'Jane Doe'`. If this expectation is not met, the test will fail, indicating that the `UserService` is not interacting with the `UserGateway` as expected."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Between Stubs and Mocks"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stubs provide predefined responses; mocks verify interactions."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stubs are used to control inputs; mocks are used to verify outputs (interactions)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stubs are simpler to implement; mocks require more setup and expectation definition."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Mockery for Mocking"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mockery is a popular mocking framework for PHP that provides a fluent and expressive API for creating and managing mock objects. It offers features like method call expectations, argument matching, and return value configuration."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example using Mockery
use Mockery;
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class PaymentServiceTest extends TestCase {
  public function testProcessPayment() {
    $paymentGateway = Mockery::mock(PaymentGateway::class);

    $paymentGateway->shouldReceive('charge')
                   ->with(100, 'USD')
                   ->once()
                   ->andReturn(true);

    $paymentService = new PaymentService($paymentGateway);
    $result = $paymentService->processPayment(100, 'USD');

    $this->assertTrue($result);
  }

  public function tearDown(): void
  {
    Mockery::close();
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we're using Mockery to create a mock `PaymentGateway` object. We're setting an expectation that the `charge` method will be called once with the arguments `100` and `'USD'`, and that it will return `true`. The `tearDown` method is important to close Mockery and prevent memory leaks."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Stubs and Mocks"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use stubs when you need to control the inputs to the code under test and verify that it behaves correctly given those inputs. Use mocks when you need to verify that the code under test interacts with its dependencies in the expected way."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider using stubs for simple dependencies and mocks for more complex interactions. Choose the tool that best suits your testing needs and helps you write clear, maintainable, and reliable tests."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mocking and stubbing are essential techniques for writing effective unit tests in PHP. By isolating the code under test and controlling its dependencies, you can create tests that are fast, reliable, and easy to understand. Understanding the differences between stubs and mocks, and choosing the right tool for the job, will help you write better tests and build more robust software."}]
